#QUESTION ENFORCER

#the pick that means the drafts questions are all good, no rewrite needed
FOLLOWS: str = "follows"

#what jev gets asked, the examples ride right under it
def question() -> str:
    return """Does a question in `draft_reply`, the shop's reply about to go out, break one of the question rules? `last_question_asked` is the last question the shop asked them ("" when it never asked one), `message_context` is the conversation before their message, and `current_customer_message` is what they just sent.
Read what they said in `current_customer_message` and `message_context` first, then judge only the asks in `draft_reply`. One ask is a question plus the words that finish it, and it always counts as one question, never two: "What car is it? Send me a pic of it if you can", "Do you have an exact orange in mind? If you do, can you shoot a pic of it over", "What car is it, and what color is it right now?" and "Do you have a photo of it as it is now, and of the look you want?" are each one question. A line that asks nothing ("just reach out whenever you're ready") is never a question, and what `draft_reply` answers, prices or says never changes the answer.
Pick follows when every question in `draft_reply` fits, or when it asks none. Otherwise pick the rule its question breaks."""

#every pick jev can make, follows or the one rule the drafts question breaks
def criteria() -> dict[str, str]:
    rules: dict[str, str] = {}

    rules[FOLLOWS] = "Every question in `draft_reply` fits, or it asks none: one ask at most, counting a question with the words that finish it as one, nothing they already gave or answered, no ask the shop never makes, no ask about what they said they're waiting on once they said they'll reach out when it comes, and the schedule question only when `last_question_asked` didn't already ask it, or the shop's last reply answered something else with no question and they only took that answer in, and they never said the vehicle isn't ready, that they're still deciding or that they'll reach out themselves. Asking for what the job still needs, or the schedule question once they're ready, fits."

    #the two the owner called out himself
    rules["schedule_asked_again"] = """`last_question_asked` already asked them to get on the schedule or the books, in any words ("Did you want to hop on the schedule?", "Want me to get you on the books?"), `current_customer_message` didn't say yes to it or ask to book, and `draft_reply` asks them to get on the schedule again, in any words. Never when the shop's last reply in `message_context` answered something they asked with no question of its own and `current_customer_message` only takes that answer in ("alright sounds good", "ok cool", "got it"), since the schedule question is the next step then."""

    rules["car_not_ready"] = "`current_customer_message` or `message_context` says the vehicle isn't with them yet or isn't ready (it's being shipped or delivered, they're still buying it, they pick it up later, it's in another shop), that they'll follow up once it's here, or that they're on their way over with it, `current_customer_message` didn't ask to book, and `draft_reply` asks them to get on the schedule or when they want to bring it in."

    rules["still_deciding"] = "`current_customer_message` or `message_context` says they're still deciding (shopping around, waiting on other quotes, thinking it over, talking it over with someone, saving up) or that they'll reach out when they're ready, nothing they said since says they're ready now, `current_customer_message` didn't ask to book, and `draft_reply` asks them to get on the schedule or book."

    #they already said they'll text once it lands, so asking if it landed yet is just hovering
    rules["wait_chased"] = """`current_customer_message` or `message_context` says they're waiting on something before they go ahead (the vehicle getting delivered or back, other quotes, talking it over, getting paid) and that they'll reach out once it comes, nothing they said since says it came or brings it up again, and `draft_reply` asks about it anyway: whether it came yet, when it's coming, or whether they heard back or decided ("Is the truck there yet?", "Any idea when it's getting delivered?", "Any word on the other quotes?")."""

    rules["already_given"] = """`draft_reply` asks for something they already gave, in `current_customer_message` or earlier in `message_context`: the vehicle (its year, make or model), the color it is now, a pic they already sent, the color they want, which service they want, the coverage or the windows. Once they typed the color it is now, asking what color it is again asks for something they gave, and so does asking for a pic of it after they typed a neutral (low saturation) one (black, white, gray, silver, beige, tan or cream, in any shade or finish) or after the shop already asked them twice for that pic. Any other color they typed for it, bright or not (red, blue, navy, green, brown and the rest), still needs a pic of their vehicle, so asking for that pic the first or second time ("Perfect, good to know it's blue, can you snap a pic of it and send it over whenever you get a chance") never asks for something they gave, and neither does asking for a pic when they couldn't name the color. A color they named for the look they want is only the idea, so asking for the exact shade as a pic or a link ("Do you have an exact white in mind? If you do, can you shoot a pic of it over") never asks for something they gave."""

    rules["already_answered"] = """`current_customer_message` already answered `last_question_asked`, a yes or a no to the schedule question included, and `draft_reply` asks it again, in the same words or in other words. A yes to the schedule question in an earlier message that came with a question of its own never counts here once the shop's last reply answered that question: asking it again when `current_customer_message` only takes that answer in ("alright sounds good") fits."""

    rules["more_than_one"] = """`draft_reply` asks two separate things that each want their own answer, like the car and then the color they want in two questions, or an add on and then the schedule. One question with the words that finish it is never more than one: "Send me a pic of it if you can" or "If you do, can you shoot a pic of it over" right after a question is part of that question, and one question grouping the pieces a price needs ("What car is it, and what color is it right now?") or a couple of pics ("Do you have a photo of it as it is now, and of the look you want?") is one question too."""

    rules["off_limits"] = "`draft_reply` asks for their email, a deposit or a payment, or asks or tells them to swing by, come in, stop by the shop or check Instagram, when `current_customer_message` didn't ask about coming in, seeing the colors or seeing past work."

    return rules

#what the regen gets told for each broken rule, short and bossy on purpose
def fixes() -> dict[str, str]:
    fix_lines: dict[str, str] = {}

    fix_lines["schedule_asked_again"] = "The draft asks them to get on the schedule again right after the shop's last question already asked it. Take the schedule question out and end the reply on the answer, with no question, and never say they're set or booked, nothing's on the schedule till they say yes to it."

    fix_lines["car_not_ready"] = "They said the vehicle isn't ready yet or that they'll follow up once it's here, and the draft asks them to book anyway. Take the schedule question out, answer what they asked, and end the reply with no question so they can reach out once it's here."

    fix_lines["still_deciding"] = "They said they're still deciding or will reach out when they're ready, and the draft asks them to book anyway. Take the schedule question out, answer what they asked, and end the reply with no question so the door stays open."

    fix_lines["wait_chased"] = "They said they'll reach out once what they're waiting on comes, and the draft asks about it anyway. Take that question out and end the reply with no question, so they can reach out once it's here."

    fix_lines["already_given"] = "The draft asks for something they already gave. Take that ask out and go by what they gave."

    fix_lines["already_answered"] = "The draft asks the shop's last question again even though their message already answered it. Take that question out and go by their answer."

    fix_lines["more_than_one"] = "The draft asks more than one question. Keep only the one that moves the job forward the most and take the rest out."

    fix_lines["off_limits"] = "The draft asks for their email, a deposit or a payment, or asks them to come in, swing by or check Instagram when they didn't ask. Take that ask out."

    return fix_lines

#tacked onto the fixes when the draft asks for a color still owed, so no regen drops that ask
def keep_color_ask(color_asks: list[str]) -> str:
    color_ask_text: str = " ".join(color_asks)

    return f"COLOR ASK: \"{color_ask_text}\" asks for a color the job still waits on, so it stays in the reply word for word. Fix only what the other lines name."

